"Traces observed physical movement over a workplace plan to reveal unnecessary travel, backtracking and handling.\n\nUse when: Improve a workshop, laboratory or service-counter layout. Compare movement before and after moving equipment or supplies.\n\nAvoid when: The problem concerns logical task order without meaningful physical locations.\n\nVisual grammar: A plan locates fixed stations and storage areas. Numbered route segments preserve the observed visit order. Separate layers distinguish people, materials or observation runs.\n\nBuild: Sketch the workplace at a usable stated scale. Observe one representative journey and record each actual stop. Annotate repeated travel and compare an alternative layout separately.\n\nCheck: The route follows observed movement and plausible access paths. Observation scope, actor and period are stated. Keep unavoidable physical route intersections in separate layers or views.\n\nPitfalls: Inventing straight shortcuts through walls or restricted areas. Combining many journeys into an unreadable tangle.\n\nExample: A packing worker repeatedly walks between cartons, labels and scales; separate movement views support relocating those supplies.\n\nAlternatives: Value-stream map, Reference map."
